BUSINESS LEADERS RICH & POWERFUL
Charlie Munger: Life, Berkshire Hathaway,
Investments
By Updated November 28, 2023ANN BEHAN
Reviewed by ERIC ESTEVEZ
Fact checked by KATRINA MUNICHIELLO
Billionaire Charlie Munger, former vice chair and second-in-command to Warren
Buffett, died on Nov. 28, 2023, at age 99. As Buffett’s closest business partner
and “right-hand man” for over four decades, Munger was instrumental in the
growth of Berkshire Hathaway into a giant diversified holding company with a
market capitalization of over $780 billion (as of November 2023) and
subsidiaries operating in insurance, freight rail transportation, energy
generation/distribution, manufacturing, and retail.
In addition to serving as independent director of Costco Wholesale Corporation,
Munger was chair of the board of Daily Journal Corporation, a Los Angeles-
based legal publisher with a software business in the automated court
reporting market. From 1984 through 2011, he served as chair and CEO of
Wesco Financial Corporation, a subsidiary of Berkshire Hathaway.
KEY TAKEAWAYS
As vice chair of the $355-billion conglomerate Berkshire Hathaway,
Charlie Munger was second-in-command to renowned investor Warren
Buffett from 1978 to 2023.
