Education and Early Career
Born in Omaha in 1924, as a teenager, Munger worked at Buffett & Son, a
grocery store owned by Warren Buffett's grandfather.
During World War II, he enrolled in the University of Michigan to study
mathematics but dropped out a few days after his 19th birthday in 1943 to
serve in the U.S. Army Air Corps, where he was trained as a meteorologist and
promoted to second lieutenant. He later continued his studies in meteorology
at Caltech in Pasadena, Calif., the town that became his lifelong home.
As Buffett’s “right-hand man,” Munger was instrumental in the growth
of Berkshire into a giant holding company with a market capitalization
of more than $780 billion.
He dropped out of the University of Michigan in 1943 to serve in the
U.S. Army Air Corps, where he was trained as a meteorologist.
Although he never earned an undergraduate degree, he graduated
magna cum laude from Harvard Law School with a J.D. in 1948.
